# Copy to `.env` and edit. Never commit the real file.

# --- Required -----------------------------------------------------------------
# URL-safe characters only (it is embedded in a connection URL), e.g.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

# Public URL people use to reach this instance.
CAMPBOOKS_PUBLIC_URL=https://campbooks.example.com

# --- Optional -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port for the web app and API (container listens on 8080).
CAMPBOOKS_PORT=8080
# json (default) or pretty
CAMPBOOKS_LOG_FORMAT=json
# Apply database migrations on startup (default true).
CAMPBOOKS_RUN_MIGRATIONS=true
# RUST_LOG=info

# --- Sign-in (Campbooks is its own identity provider, ADR 0014) --------------------
# Nothing to set for the issuer: leave CAMPBOOKS_AUTH__ISSUER unset. People sign in
# with Google, Microsoft, Zoho or a passkey; register your OAuth apps and set the
# CAMPBOOKS_GOOGLE__*, CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__* and CAMPBOOKS_ZOHO__* settings below
# (README.md -> "Built-in sign-in"). The client secrets are files in deploy/secrets/.
#
# Only for an outside OpenID Connect issuer (advanced, not provisioned for you):
# CAMPBOOKS_AUTH__ISSUER, CAMPBOOKS_AUTH__JWKS_URL, CAMPBOOKS_AUTH__AUDIENCE and
# CAMPBOOKS_AUTH__LEEWAY_SECONDS.

# --- Workspaces and signup -----------------------------------------------------
# Who may create a workspace when they belong to none yet: open (default),
# invite_only (people join by invitation), approval (requests are recorded;
# nobody is let in until approvals exist), or code (a signup code is needed).
# The instance's first user may always create one. Also decides whether the
# sign-in providers (Google, Microsoft, Zoho) create accounts for new people.
# CAMPBOOKS_SIGNUP__POLICY=open
# Comma-separated codes accepted under `code` (each reusable). Secret.
# CAMPBOOKS_SIGNUP__CODES=
# Lock the whole instance to your own people: comma-separated addresses
# (ana@example.com) and domains (example.com, exactly: not its subdomains).
# Unset or empty means no restriction. When set, nobody else can sign in or
# create an account, existing accounts included, whatever the policy above
# says and even with an invitation; an account with no listed, provider-verified
# address (a passkey-only one) cannot sign in either. Tightening it cuts access
# at the next token refresh (within minutes). A malformed entry stops startup.
# CAMPBOOKS_SIGNUP__ALLOWED=
# `development` logs invitation links (nothing emails them yet). Never set it
# on a server other people use.
# CAMPBOOKS_ENV=production

# --- Database logins ------------------------------------------------------------
# The bundled compose file already wires these up (nothing to set): the server
# queries as a dedicated `campbooks_runtime` login created by `db-init`, and the
# owner login (POSTGRES_PASSWORD) only applies migrations. For your own
# Postgres, see "Database logins" in deploy/README.md:
#   CAMPBOOKS_DATABASE_URL            runtime login (non-superuser)
#   CAMPBOOKS_DATABASE_PASSWORD_FILE  file holding that login's password (optional)
#   CAMPBOOKS_DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL  owner login, used only to run migrations
# With a single privileged URL the server switches each connection to this
# role (row-level security applies to it) and warns; leave the default unless
# your database administrator created another one for you.
# CAMPBOOKS_DATABASE_ROLE=campbooks_app
# Start even though the database login bypasses row-level security (the
# server refuses otherwise). Workspace isolation then rests on queries alone.
# CAMPBOOKS_DATABASE_ALLOW_RLS_BYPASS=false

# --- Rotating the instance secret key ------------------------------------------------
# While CAMPBOOKS_SECRET_KEY is being replaced, the old key opens (and verifies)
# what it sealed, which is sealed again with the new key as it is used. Remove it
# after 30 days. Never set it to a key that leaked. See README.md -> "Rotating
# the instance secret key".
# CAMPBOOKS_SECRET_KEY_PREVIOUS_FILE=/run/campbooks/secret-key-previous

# --- Web sign-in (the BFF, ADR 0004) ----------------------------------------------
# The server signs browsers in through Campbooks' own issuer and encrypts the
# tokens it keeps with CAMPBOOKS_SECRET_KEY (generated into the `server-secrets`
# volume by server-init). All optional; defaults shown.
# Sessions end after this long unused (minutes) ...
# CAMPBOOKS_BFF__IDLE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=2880
# ... and this long after sign-in at the latest (hours, at most 8784: 366 days).
# CAMPBOOKS_BFF__ABSOLUTE_TIMEOUT_HOURS=720
# A session's cookie value is replaced this often (minutes).
# CAMPBOOKS_BFF__ROTATE_MINUTES=60
# How long a started sign-in may take at the identity provider (seconds).
# CAMPBOOKS_BFF__LOGIN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
# Scopes to request (default: openid profile email offline_access + every API scope).
# CAMPBOOKS_BFF__SCOPES=

# --- Behind a reverse proxy --------------------------------------------------------
# The proxies whose X-Forwarded-For names the client: comma-separated addresses or
# CIDR blocks of whatever connects to the server (e.g. the Docker network your proxy
# reaches it over: `docker network inspect <network> -f '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Subnet}}{{end}}'`).
# Unset, the socket peer is the client: behind a proxy that makes every client one,
# and the sign-in rate limits (per address) then throttle everyone together.
# CAMPBOOKS_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.18.0.0/16

# --- API rate limits per caller ---------------------------------------------------
# Every call with a token is counted per person and app (an app on its own per
# client id; automation and the assistant apart from the person's apps). Over a
# limit: 429 `rate_limited` with Retry-After. A count of 0 turns that class off.
# Reads, writes and admin calls are counted per server process (with several API
# processes a caller may get that many times the budget); sends across all of them.
# All optional; defaults shown.
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__ENABLED=true
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__READS=600
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__READS_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__WRITES=120
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__WRITES_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# Mail sends, per person across all their apps.
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__SENDS=30
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__SENDS_WINDOW_SECONDS=600
# The admin API, per administrator.
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__ADMIN=120
# CAMPBOOKS_RATE_LIMITS__ADMIN_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# --- Connected accounts (connectors, ADR 0010) -----------------------------------
# Members connect their Gmail with Google's consent; Campbooks keeps the grant
# encrypted with CAMPBOOKS_SECRET_KEY and syncs mail adaptively. Off until you
# register a Google OAuth client (see "Connected accounts" in deploy/README.md).
# The same client signs people in with Google when Campbooks is its own identity
# provider (CAMPBOOKS_AUTH__ISSUER unset; see "Built-in sign-in" in the README):
# CAMPBOOKS_GOOGLE__CLIENT_ID=
# CAMPBOOKS_GOOGLE__CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/campbooks/google-client-secret
# Only if your OAuth app already registered a different sign-in callback path and a
# reverse proxy rewrites it to /auth/callback/google (see "Built-in sign-in" in the
# README). Absolute path, no host or query. Default: /auth/callback/google.
# CAMPBOOKS_GOOGLE__REDIRECT_PATH=/oauth/gmail/callback
# Sign in with Microsoft (personal, work and school accounts) and Zoho (every data
# centre) with the built-in sign-in; see "Built-in sign-in" in the README. Redirect
# URIs: /auth/callback/microsoft and /auth/callback/zoho (or a REDIRECT_PATH as above).
# CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__CLIENT_ID=
# CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/campbooks/microsoft-client-secret
# common (default), organizations, consumers, or one tenant id.
# CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__TENANT=common
# CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__REDIRECT_PATH=/oauth/microsoft/callback
# CAMPBOOKS_ZOHO__CLIENT_ID=
# CAMPBOOKS_ZOHO__CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/campbooks/zoho-client-secret
# The accounts server of the data centre your Zoho app is registered in, e.g.
# https://accounts.zoho.eu for an app created in the EU console. Leave it at the
# default only if the app has Multi-DC enabled. One of accounts.zoho.com (default),
# .eu, .in, .com.au, .jp, .ca, .sa, .uk, written as https://<host> with no path.
# CAMPBOOKS_ZOHO__ACCOUNTS_SERVER=https://accounts.zoho.com
# CAMPBOOKS_ZOHO__REDIRECT_PATH=/oauth/zoho/callback
# Apps that register themselves with the built-in sign-in, for MCP agents that
# connect without anyone setting them up first. Both off by default: turn them on
# only if people here use such agents. Every such app still needs a person's yes
# on the consent screen, is marked unverified, and can read only one workspace.
# Dynamic client registration (RFC 7591) at /oauth2/register:
# CAMPBOOKS_OAUTH__DYNAMIC_REGISTRATION=off
# An https URL as a client id, its description fetched from there (public
# addresses only):
# CAMPBOOKS_OAUTH__CLIENT_METADATA_DOCUMENTS=off
# All optional; defaults shown.
# How far back a new Gmail connection reads (days).
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__GMAIL__BACKFILL_DAYS=90
# Messages larger than this (bytes) are skipped and recorded as oversized.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__GMAIL__MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=52428800
# One sync run stops starting downloads after this many bytes and continues
# in the next run, which bounds its memory.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__GMAIL__RUN_BUDGET_BYTES=67108864
# Polling: the fastest interval after changes, and the slowest when idle (seconds).
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__POLL__MIN_SECONDS=60
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__POLL__MAX_SECONDS=14400
# Google API quota units per second, shared by every server process. Every unit
# a sync spends is counted, whatever the rate: a low rate only slows big pushes.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__GOOGLE__UNITS_PER_SECOND=5000
# Calendar sync: the requests each connection's calendars may make per minute
# (shared by every process), and Zoho Calendar, off by default (its adapter is
# not yet verified against a live Zoho grant). Google Calendar needs its API
# enabled on the Google project this server is registered as.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__CALENDAR__REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=300
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__ZOHO_CALENDAR=false
# Zoho Mail: the window a new mailbox reads, its size limits, the requests each
# Zoho login may make per minute (shared by every process), and how often a
# mailbox's whole window is re-read (Zoho has no change log).
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__ZOHO__BACKFILL_DAYS=90
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__ZOHO__MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=52428800
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__ZOHO__RUN_BUDGET_BYTES=67108864
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__ZOHO__REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=60
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__ZOHO__SWEEP_MINUTES=360
# Outlook and Microsoft 365 mail through Microsoft Graph, with the app of Sign in
# with Microsoft (CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__* above; see "Connected accounts" in the
# README). Off by default; while off nothing below is read. TENANT is where the
# mail consent goes and its tokens are refreshed: common (personal, work and school
# accounts), organizations, consumers, or one tenant id. Unset, it is sign-in's
# CAMPBOOKS_MICROSOFT__TENANT, so a single-tenant app never widens.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__MICROSOFT__ENABLED=false
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__MICROSOFT__TENANT=common
# Any mailbox over IMAP and SMTP, connected with its password (on by
# default; needs the instance secret key: without it IMAP is not offered, and
# setting ENABLED=true without it stops the server). Its servers are reached
# at public addresses on ports 993, 143, 465 and 587 only, unless a
# self-hosted instance allows its own network or any port.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__ENABLED=true
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS=false
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__ALLOW_ANY_PORT=false
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# Reading IMAP mailboxes: how far back a new one reads (days), the largest
# message downloaded and the bytes one run may receive, minutes between sweeps
# of a mailbox's flags, sessions open at once per IMAP host (all processes;
# a big provider's IMAP host, from a short list kept in the code, takes
# PROVIDER_SESSIONS_PER_HOST instead) and how many of them one workspace may
# hold, and the most folders read per mailbox.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__BACKFILL_DAYS=90
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=52428800
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__RUN_BUDGET_BYTES=67108864
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SWEEP_MINUTES=360
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SESSIONS_PER_HOST=4
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__PROVIDER_SESSIONS_PER_HOST=200
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__WORKSPACE_SESSIONS_PER_HOST=2
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__MAX_FOLDERS=200
# Push with IDLE (off by default; needs IMAP on): each worker holds up to
# IDLE_SESSIONS mailboxes' Inboxes in IDLE (IDLE_SESSIONS_PER_WORKSPACE of
# them for one workspace at most), asks for at most one run per
# IDLE_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS per mailbox, renews IDLE every IDLE_REFRESH_MINUTES
# (at most 29), lets a mailbox quiet for IDLE_QUIET_DAYS go back to polling,
# polls a mailbox held in IDLE at most every IDLE_POLL_SECONDS (a safety
# poll), and lets one workspace hold at most WORKSPACE_IDLE_SESSIONS_PER_HOST
# IDLE sessions on one host (on a small host, at most half its share).
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_ENABLED=false
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_SESSIONS=50
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_SESSIONS_PER_WORKSPACE=5
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS=30
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_REFRESH_MINUTES=25
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_QUIET_DAYS=14
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__IDLE_POLL_SECONDS=900
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__WORKSPACE_IDLE_SESSIONS_PER_HOST=10
# Members' changes are made at the IMAP server (read, starred, archive,
# trash, spam, streams); a stream with no place of its own gets a
# Campbooks/<Stream> folder, at most MAX_CREATED_FOLDERS per mailbox.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__MAX_CREATED_FOLDERS=10
# Sending through an IMAP mailbox's SMTP server (checked only while IMAP is
# on): messages a mailbox login sends an hour, recipients one message may
# have and a login sends to a day, messages a workspace and an SMTP host (all
# workspaces) send an hour, the largest message sent (bytes, at most 35 MiB)
# and how long one submission may take (seconds, at most 600). A big
# provider's SMTP host (a short list kept in the code) has no host cap; on any
# other host one workspace takes at most half of SEND_HOST_PER_HOUR.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_PER_HOUR=50
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_RECIPIENTS_PER_MESSAGE=50
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_RECIPIENTS_PER_DAY=500
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_WORKSPACE_PER_HOUR=200
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_HOST_PER_HOUR=1000
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=26214400
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__IMAP__SEND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
# Failed runs in a row before a connection shows as failing.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__FAILING_AFTER=5
# Days a produced change batch is kept for replay.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONNECTORS__BATCH_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# --- Background jobs ------------------------------------------------------------
# The server runs the job worker next to the API (`campbooks-server all`, the
# image's default). All optional; defaults shown. Each running job may hold a
# database connection, so keep CAMPBOOKS_DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS (default 10)
# above the concurrency.
# Jobs one process runs at once.
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__CONCURRENCY=4
# Jobs of one workspace that may run at once per queue (fairness).
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__PER_WORKSPACE=2
# Idle poll; new jobs wake the worker at once anyway.
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__POLL_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# How long succeeded jobs are kept.
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__RETENTION_HOURS=168
# How long failed jobs are kept for inspection.
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__FAILED_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# How long the event log keeps what happened (ids, never mail). Replaying a
# consumer reaches back this far; undelivered events are never pruned.
# CAMPBOOKS_EVENTS__RETENTION_DAYS=90
# How long a stopping worker waits for running jobs (keep below the stop timeout).
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__SHUTDOWN_GRACE_SECONDS=8
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__LEASE_SECONDS=60
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__RETRY_BASE_MS=1000
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__RETRY_MAX_SECONDS=3600
# Where a worker-only process answers its health probe (`healthcheck --worker`).
# The probe always dials 127.0.0.1, so keep this on loopback (only the port matters).
# CAMPBOOKS_JOBS__HEALTH_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8081

# --- Change feed (live updates in the app) --------------------------------------
# Clients follow a stream of what changed (Server-Sent Events). A reverse proxy
# in front of the server must not buffer `text/event-stream` responses (the
# server sends `X-Accel-Buffering: no` for nginx) and should allow idle
# connections longer than the heartbeat. All optional; defaults shown.
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=25
# A stream ends after this long (or when its access token expires) and the
# client reconnects from where it was.
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__MAX_CONNECTION_SECONDS=900
# Idle poll; changes wake streams at once anyway.
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__POLL_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# How long changes are kept for clients that were away; a client away
# longer refetches what it shows.
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__RETENTION_HOURS=72
# Open streams per person (or API client) per server process.
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__MAX_STREAMS_PER_PRINCIPAL=8
# Streams a person (or API client) may open per window, across processes
# (0 turns it off).
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__OPENS_PER_WINDOW=30
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__OPENS_WINDOW_SECONDS=60  (1 to 86400)
# A stream catching up sends one page per tick, on the tick, so when a page
# leaves says nothing about changes the caller may not see.
# CAMPBOOKS_CHANGES__CATCH_UP_TICK_MS=50  (50 to 1000)

# --- Blob storage (message bodies, attachments, files) ---------------------------
# Stored outside Postgres, once per workspace however many messages carry them.
# Default: the `blobs-data` volume mounted at /data/blobs (local disk).
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__BACKEND=fs
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__PATH=/data/blobs
# Largest file accepted, in bytes (default 50 MiB).
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__MAX_OBJECT_BYTES=52428800
# How long a file nothing uses any more is kept before it is deleted (hours).
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__SWEEP_GRACE_HOURS=24
#
# Or any S3-compatible store (AWS S3, Garage, MinIO, Ceph, R2, ...). Create the
# bucket first; keep it private. See README.md -> Blob storage.
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__BACKEND=s3
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__S3__BUCKET=campbooks-blobs
# Unset for AWS; http:// is accepted for a store on a private network.
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__S3__ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.com
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__S3__REGION=us-east-1
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__S3__ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__S3__SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# `true` for <endpoint>/<bucket> URLs (most self-hosted stores); `false` (default)
# for <bucket>.<endpoint>.
# CAMPBOOKS_BLOBS__S3__PATH_STYLE=true

# --- Outgoing mail (off by default) ---------------------------------------------
# The instance's own mail: a notice to the account when a way to sign in is added.
# Unset, nothing is sent and the notice is shown in the app only. See README.md ->
# Outgoing mail. Both or neither. The URL carries the password: secret (or put it
# in a file and set CAMPBOOKS_MAIL__SMTP_URL_FILE instead). TLS is required:
# plain smtp:// (or tls=opportunistic) is refused, except to a relay on this
# machine (127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost) given no user or password.
# Implicit TLS (port 465):
# CAMPBOOKS_MAIL__SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com
# STARTTLS (port 587):
# CAMPBOOKS_MAIL__SMTP_URL=smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587?tls=required
# CAMPBOOKS_MAIL__FROM=Campbooks <no-reply@example.com>

# --- Monitoring (off by default) ------------------------------------------------
# See README.md -> Monitoring. Prometheus metrics at /metrics on a separate port;
# no authentication, so keep it on the Docker network (it is not published).
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__METRICS_ADDR=0.0.0.0:9464
# Traces and metrics over OTLP/HTTP to a collector (/v1/traces and /v1/metrics
# are appended).
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://collector:4318
# Headers for the collector, key=value pairs separated by commas. Secret.
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__OTLP_HEADERS=
# Share of new traces kept, 0 to 1 (default 1).
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__SAMPLE_RATIO=1
# Error reports (ids and error kinds only) to a Sentry-compatible tracker such
# as GlitchTip. Secret. Check with `campbooks-server telemetry test-error`.
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__ERROR_DSN=https://key@errors.example.com/1
# The environment error reports and the app's telemetry are filed under.
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__ENVIRONMENT=production
# The app's own errors and web vitals, scrubbed and forwarded by the server to
# a Grafana Faro receiver (Alloy faro.receiver). The key is sent as x-api-key.
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__CLIENT_COLLECTOR_URL=http://alloy:12347/collect
# CAMPBOOKS_TELEMETRY__CLIENT_COLLECTOR_KEY=

# --- Usage analytics (off by default) -------------------------------------------
# See README.md -> Usage analytics. The screens the app shows, as route templates
# (/w/:workspaceId/inbox), relayed by the server to a Matomo site; no script in
# the app, no IP, a visitor id that changes every day. Off unless all three are
# set. The visitor key is a secret of at least 32 characters
# (`openssl rand -base64 32`).
# CAMPBOOKS_ANALYTICS__MATOMO_URL=https://analytics.example.com
# CAMPBOOKS_ANALYTICS__MATOMO_SITE_ID=1
# CAMPBOOKS_ANALYTICS__MATOMO_VISITOR_KEY=

# --- Tracked events (off by default) --------------------------------------------
# The record of every action (docs/tracking.md, ADR 0020): who did what and a
# snapshot of the records it touched, ids and counts only, never mail. `none`
# records nothing; `outbox` writes each event to the database table
# kernel.tracked_events in the transaction of the change and keeps it there;
# `amqp` also publishes it to your RabbitMQ (see README.md -> Tracked events).
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__SINK=none
# With `amqp`: the broker (secret; amqps:// for TLS) and, optionally, where to
# publish. Turn DECLARE_TOPOLOGY off if the broker user may not configure.
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__AMQP_URL=amqp://campbooks:change-me@rabbitmq:5672/%2f
# Or leave the password out of the URL and read it from a file (it replaces
# any password in the URL):
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__AMQP_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/mq/relay-password
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__EXCHANGE=campbooks.events
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__STREAM=campbooks.events
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__STREAM_MAX_AGE=30D
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__DECLARE_TOPOLOGY=true
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__BATCH_SIZE=200
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__POLL_INTERVAL_MS=1000
# CAMPBOOKS_TRACKING__PUBLISHED_RETENTION_HOURS=24

# --- AI roles -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Every AI call goes through a role. A role left unset is simply unavailable.
# The values below match `docker compose --profile ai`; for a hosted provider
# serving open-weight models, set its URL, model and CAMPBOOKS_AI__<ROLE>__API_KEY.
#
# Mail stays on your network by default. A role whose URL is outside this
# instance's network gets no mail content unless you opt in for that role:
#   CAMPBOOKS_AI__<ROLE>__EXTERNAL_CONTENT=allow
# Without it, Campbooks refuses to send it anything but the health probe, and
# there is no decide pass: mail stays in Primary and only the facts' "no"
# (list mail, automatic mail, no-reply senders need no reply) acts. "This instance's network" means
# a loopback or private address (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, fc00::/7),
# localhost, a single-label name (a compose service such as `laya` or
# `ollama`), or a host you list here (exact names, or `.domain` for a whole
# domain). Campbooks never consults DNS for this check, but the request itself
# is resolved by the host: a single-label name counts as "this network" only
# if your resolver keeps it local. Make sure no DNS search domain can resolve
# such a name (`laya`, `ollama`) to an outside address; inside docker compose
# the service names always win. Redirects from AI endpoints are never
# followed. Admin -> System shows where each role is.
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__INTERNAL_HOSTS=gpu.example.internal,.lan.example.com

# Daily AI limit: model calls each workspace may make per UTC day, every role
# together (today one decide call per incoming email). Past it, new mail is
# still stored and sorted by the facts and taught rules, with no model pass,
# until 00:00 UTC; it is not asked again later. A tenth of the limit is kept
# for what people ask Scout themselves. One line is logged when a
# workspace reaches it, and the admin API (GET /admin/v1/system/ai-usage)
# shows each workspace's day. Default 5000; 0 for no limit (your own model).
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DAILY_CALLS_PER_WORKSPACE=5000

# decide: typed decisions (which stream, does it ask something, how urgent),
# answered with probabilities (ADR 0009). Reading what an email says is the
# decide model's job: without one, nothing is sorted out of Primary and no
# need is raised beyond what the headers settle (ADR 0018). A model's answers
# are used only while it passes its answers check (Admin -> System).
# Default: Laya multilingual, served by the `laya` service. Its key is
# required: the `laya` service refuses to start without it, and the server
# sends it. Generate one with
#   openssl rand -hex 32
CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__PROTOCOL=laya
CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__BASE_URL=http://laya:8000/v1
CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__MODEL=convaiinnovations/laya-multilingual
CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__API_KEY=
# Where decide requests are posted, relative to BASE_URL (default `systemone`
# for PROTOCOL=laya, `chat/completions` for openai).
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__PATH=
# Or Jev, a hosted decision model (closed, hosted in the US) speaking the same
# protocol through OpenRouter (ADR 0018). Every email's sender, recipients,
# subject, list headers, attachment names and first 1,500 characters then
# leave this instance for OpenRouter and TypeSafe: turn it on only after
# reading their region, retention and processing terms. The opt-in below is
# what allows it.
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__PROTOCOL=laya
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/alpha
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__PATH=decisions
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.13
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__API_KEY=
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__EXTERNAL_CONTENT=allow
# Or an instruct model answering the same typed questions (JSON-schema output;
# probabilities from token logprobs when the server returns them). PROTOCOL
# defaults to openai, so settings from before the typed protocol keep working:
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__PROTOCOL=openai
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__MODEL=qwen3:8b
# Set to false for servers that reject the logprobs parameter (default true).
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__LOGPROBS=true
# Calibration: a temperature per question type, optionally per option count,
# applied to decide's probabilities (default 1.0; above 1 makes them less
# sure). Will be refitted from users' corrections later.
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__CALIBRATION__NOUL=2.0
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__CALIBRATION__CHOICE=1.5
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__CALIBRATION__CHOICE_2=1.9
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__DECIDE__CALIBRATION__SCORE=1.25
# Mail sent longer ago than this many days gets no decide call (so importing an
# old mailbox costs no AI); the facts still read every message's headers.
# CAMPBOOKS_CONVERSATIONS__DECIDE_BACKFILL_DAYS=14

# complete: longer generation (drafts, summaries)
CAMPBOOKS_AI__COMPLETE__BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
CAMPBOOKS_AI__COMPLETE__MODEL=qwen3:8b
# How much a reasoning model thinks before it answers: minimal, low, medium or
# high, sent as `reasoning: {"effort": ...}` on every chat call of the role.
# Unset (the default) sends nothing. Only for a provider that documents the
# `reasoning` object (OpenRouter does); a strict OpenAI-compatible server
# rejects it. Any role takes it: CAMPBOOKS_AI__<ROLE>__REASONING_EFFORT.
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__COMPLETE__REASONING_EFFORT=low
# embed: search vectors (TEI serving BAAI/bge-m3, 1024 dims).
# On arm64: http://ollama:11434/v1 with model bge-m3.
CAMPBOOKS_AI__EMBED__BASE_URL=http://embeddings:80/v1
CAMPBOOKS_AI__EMBED__MODEL=BAAI/bge-m3
# vision (optional): images and scanned documents
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__VISION__BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# CAMPBOOKS_AI__VISION__MODEL=qwen2.5vl:7b
